From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 09:17:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] dcomp: Coast, overshoot and clamp an InteractionTracker as
 Windows does.

Measured frame by frame on Windows 11 build 29671 with tools/trackerprobe
(inertia.c, clamp.c, requestid.c):

- Inertia decays at the decay rate until it goes slower than 30 pixels a
  second, and so rests (v - 30) / k from where it started; scale stops
  below 0.05 a second and goes no faster than 5.  A bound in the way is
  overshot and a damped spring brings it back onto it.  The modified
  resting position is always reported: a modifier's, or the natural one
  within the bounds.  Velocity a request adds is no impulse.
- A custom animation goes past the bounds, to where it goes; then the
  tracker settles back, as inertia for the same request with no
  velocity, its natural resting position where it is and a modified one
  of zero.
- An idle tracker is clamped to its bounds as they are, whatever the
  clamping option, and keeps no memory of a position asked for beyond
  them; bounds that push it tell the owner, with the latest request's
  id.  The memory, added for Word's in-place activation, is gone: with
  the rest of this, Excel's in-place window stays shown through Word's
  scroll animation (tools/officeautomationprobe, embedevents).
- The scale decay rate, inertia modifiers and position adjustments take
  a request id, as the position decay rate and scale bounds do.

The test checks the overshoot of a custom animation, the settling and
the natural and modified resting positions; on Windows it has no
failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

